morning from glasgow i'm going to put  ashna under pressure because this  communique has literally just dropped  within the last few seconds as we're  bringing you uh the headlines here at  sky news is there anything you give us  at this stage or do you want me to come  back i can give you one  one point that is a positive so far so  what we were waiting for was the news  about the fossil fuel subsidies whether  that was remaining within the text so in  the last literally few seconds uh i can  read out that yes point 36 it is in  there uh countries need to accelerate  efforts towards the phase out of  unabated coal power and inefficient  fossil fuel subsidies recognizing the  need for support towards a just  transition i guess to greener future  economies that is significant because so  what does that mean so that means for  the first time in the united nations  document of this kind it looks as though  the fossil fuels and coal specific terms  remain within this document now it  survived the culling of a num of a  number of days now because they've been  going over this draft document over a  number of over a number of hours over  the last few hours but there was  pressure from countries like saudi  arabia to take that mention out or water  down that language even more we know  countries within the european union but  also other nations have been putting  pressure on them to make sure that  remains in the fact that that sticking  point of fossil fuel subsidies remains  within there is significant okay and  guessing australia had a view on that as  well um anna thomas people will be  joining us in just a second um who else  wanted it taken out uh countries like  australia countries like china those big  big economies that rely so heavily on  fossil fuels like coal that some of the  biggest polluters in the world now are  having to well i guess it's written in  within this text this draft text we need  to make that clear but it is the near  final agreement that it looks as though  they will need to make some quite strong  commitments on phasing these things out  unabated coal power of course  means coal that isn't a carbon that's  not being offset so it looks as though  at the moment that that remains in but  as i say dropping within the last few  seconds we have heard from alex sharma's  um special advisor this morning as well  yeah we have a rather cryptic uh tweet  from alex sharma the uk cop president's  advisor saying is that the sun rising on  a cop 26 outcome  big day fingers crossed emoji two how  much we can read into that remains to be  seen but a lovely picture of glass let's  see  hannah thomas peter is with us um hi  hannah what's your interpretation  okay so we're just hammering  it's been cracking through the textbook  yes just literally dropped just top of  the app okay so what i noticed the first  thing that everyone goes to is to try  and find this fossil fuel language is  it's still in the text yes it is and  it's unchanged now that's a surprise  because the brits and america  were saying  really clearly all the way through  it's it's not going to survive these  negotiations it was almost a sacrificial  lamb it had been it had been put up in  the text in order for other big  polluters to fight to take it out so  that they might agree to other things  okay but it's survived the nighttime  negotiations that's really significant  optically in particular i think john  kerry is your interpretation of that  because we're just hearing from ashna  that you said people like saudi arabia  or australia wanted it to be taken out  why they why were they so desperate to  get rid of it well because it specified  the  the ending of  unabated coal use and inefficient fossil  fuel subsidies and it wasn't so much  about the precise wording but just  crossing a threshold of actually having  a mention of fossil fuels in a cop text  this is the first time it's ever  happened as mad as that sounds  that's why it's so precedent setting and  that is why the uk and the us and others  who've got ambitious carbon reduction  targets will see that as a huge win if  it stays because this might not be the  final version just looking at other  things that that remained  there has been no change to the language  around adaptation finance  so obviously there's been an agreement  that's the amount of money available for  poorer countries to adapt to the changes  that are already happening so just to  clarify that it was a hundred billion  dollars a year they were supposed to be  reaching that by 2021 then they said it  would get to 2023 then maybe 2026 where  are we with that so  that's there the 100 billion is there  it's it is going to get there by 2023.  uh the poorer countries have been very  cross about it think of it as a betrayal  but there's nothing that can you know  that i don't think that countries are  going to get there any sooner than 2022  2023 and that actually hasn't been on  the table the adaptation piece of that  hundred billion this is all very  technical but actually to poorer nations  it really matters  is how much of that hundred billion and  whatever else the rest of the world  provides how much of that is available  for things like moving communities away  from rising sea levels better protecting  critical infrastructure nothing to do  with reducing carbon emissions but  keeping people and economies safe and  it's the countries that have the least  to spend on that stuff and at least to  cause the climate crisis who have been  so desperate for the proper amounts of  funding to keep themselves safe and not  only to survive but to thrive so  that adaptation language is still there  it calls on developed nations to at  least double the adaptation finance  the next bit and this is the other  really critical bit that the uk in  particular was really fighting to keep  is that it calls on all countries who  have signed the paris agreement to come  back with more ambitious carbon  reduction targets by the end of next  year  that's much sooner than 2025 which was  the previous agreement now it's that bit  of the whole text that the uk and others  believe will close the gap between what  has been promised and what is necessary  to keep 1.5 within reach which is the  overarching aim of this whole summit so  that's also a big win so the two big  questions are is it something that will  get over the line will all signs agree  to it and if so is it enough to keep 1.5  alive  simon steele who is the climate minister  for grenada and a central voice at cop  he's been in all the rooms he stood next  to barack obama when they did their  photo photo op you know because kind of  central figure  told me yesterday that this text as it  stands  is just about enough to keep 1.5 within  reach if it's weakened any further he  said  it won't be but it's like right on the  line it's bare minimum he said so my  interpretation having just seen the text  is that it is the same if not slightly  stronger as it was  yesterday and therefore cop will be able  to say if this text survives through the  next 12 hours or so that 1.5 has been  kept within reach the other thing to say  that was causing real trouble in  negotiating rooms yesterday was the loss  and damage piece of this which is a  separate bit  of you know of funding and that is that  poorer nations have said for a long time  that  they deserve repayment for  climate disasters uh that the loss and  damage that they have suffered  historically as a result of a climate  crisis that they did not cause  needs to be addressed now america in  particular has been very resistant to  being on the hook for an open-ended  amount of money  but poorer nations have said  it's central to to our agreement here we  will not  give you our agreement we'll withhold it  if you don't address loss and damage now  what hasn't happened in this text is  there's a kind of grand plan to start  shelling out cash to small or vulnerable  nations who've been really hit hard by  hurricanes  but it's opened the door  to further discussions which is all  those nations were asking for okay  ladies my goodness it's literally just  dropped properly put you on the spot and  of course you delivered with aplomb  we'll speak to you both again very  shortly thank you very much indeed  thanks a lot

